================================================================================

USER REQUEST
  - "find dupes of files and offers to remove the worst one".

WHAT THE LIBRARY ACTUALLY HAS (surveyed ~/Music first)
  - Same YouTube video saved twice: "Title [id].mp3" (current yt-dlp) next to
    "Title-id.mp3" (older default); also mp3 next to the original .webm.
  - Artist/title swapped: "Stayin' Alive-Why Mona" vs "why mona - Stayin' Alive".
  - Track numbers / tags / file names: "03 Candy Bars [id].mp3", "03 Candy Bars.wma"
    (tagged Grand Buffet), "Grand Buffet - Candy Bars.mp3".
  - Covers that must NOT match: "Stayin' Alive (Gospel & Choir)".

MATCHING  (src/library/Duplicates.*)
  - SameBytes: files sharing a size are SHA-1'd; identical → group.
  - SameVideo: YouTube id at the END of the name, "[id]" or "-id". An 11-letter
    plain word is not an id — "[Arrangement]" had grouped two different Castlevania
    tracks in the first dry run.
  - SameSong: sorted unique words of artist+title (any order), after dropping the id,
    leading track numbers and brackets with noise words (official, video, audio,
    lyrics, hd, 4k, visualizer, remaster(ed), stereo, …); lengths within
    max(2.5 s, 1 %). Artist comes from tags, else "Artist - Title" in the file name.
    An untagged title attaches to a named copy only if exactly ONE artist fits —
    otherwise two artists' same-named songs chained into one group (test caught it).
  - Union-find merges the three; each group remembers which reasons applied.

RANKING
  - GstDiscoverer per candidate (only group members, ~160 files): codec, audio
    bitrate, sample rate, channels, video stream (cover art excluded), duration.
    TagLib bitrate fallback; size/duration estimate (marked ~) only without video.
  - Best quality: lossless > lossy bitrate × codec factor (opus 1.6, aac 1.3, vorbis
    1.25, mp3 1.0, wma 0.9). Copies > 5 s shorter than the longest = cut-off
    download, ×0.3 / never kept. A video stream costs a point (tie-break).
  - Smallest file: smallest non-truncated copy.
  - Ties: tagged copy, then the one with more plays/rating.

REMOVAL  (Library::removeDuplicates, Collection schema v2)
  - QFile::moveToTrash — never a hard delete. A file that can't be trashed is
    reported and nothing about it changes.
  - Keeper gets the plays added, the higher rating, the later Last Played, the
    earlier Date Added. Playlists are repointed to the keeper (a repeat in the same
    playlist collapses to its first spot).
  - New tombstones table (path, size, removed_at, reason, kept_path): phone sync
    will read it so a two-way merge doesn't copy removed files back from the phone.
  - Migration v1 → v2 verified on a copy of the real database (playlists intact).

UI  (ui/DuplicatesDialog, Tools › Find Duplicates…)
  - Group header (name, copies, why); rows with checkbox (checked = remove), keep/
    remove, sound ("MP3 · 127 kbps · 48 kHz"), length, size, plays. Same-song-only
    groups dimmed, and can be hidden ("Include same-song matches"). Keep: best
    quality / smallest file re-picks instantly. A group can never be fully checked.
    Double-click previews; right-click Play / Show in Folder. Confirm before Trash.
  - `cyanbeat --list-duplicates` prints the report (keeper marked *).
  - QSS: visible check boxes, combo box, progress bar.

DRY RUN ON THE REAL LIBRARY (read-only, scratch database)
  - 6 s, 76 groups, 85 files pre-checked, 637 MB. Spot-checked: RAT TAXI keeps the
    140 kbps Opus .webm over a 64 kbps mp3; Candy Bars keeps the 192 kbps WMA; the
    Castlevania false positive is gone. Some same-title pairs are different uploads
    of the same song (two "Hot Stuff" ids) — that's what the dimming is for.
  - Nothing was removed from ~/Music.

TESTS
  - duplicates_test (20 checks): id styles, the [Arrangement] case, swaps, covers,
    different artists, live versions, mp3+webm, scoring order, truncation.
  - collection_test: removal → Trash, stats merge, playlist repoint + de-repeat,
    tombstone, rescan drops it, unknown path reported. Its Trash lives in the
    temp dir (an earlier run left a file in /tmp/.Trash-1000; cleaned up).
